<!--
    A byte of a stack local saved, then overwritten by a wider write of the
    same local, then stored elsewhere: P6 merge, Cover intersection of
    overlapping pieces.  Source (gcc 11 -O0 -fno-stack-protector
    -fcf-protection=none):
        unsigned long swapbytes(unsigned long x) {
          unsigned long st[2]; char t;
          st[0] = x * 0x9e3779b97f4a7c15UL; st[1] = x >> 3;
          if (x & 1) {
            t = *((char *)&st[0] + 5);
            *((char *)&st[0] + 5) = *((char *)&st[0] + 7);
            *((char *)&st[0] + 7) = t;
            st[1] |= (st[1] & 63) << 4;
            t = *((char *)&st[0] + 6);
            *((char *)&st[0] + 6) = *((char *)&st[0] + 1);
            *((char *)&st[0] + 1) = t;
            t = *((char *)&st[0] + 1);
            *((char *)&st[0] + 1) = *((char *)&st[0] + 4);
            *((char *)&st[0] + 4) = t;
          }
          return st[0] ^ st[1];
        }
    The old byte 5 of st[0] is read after the 6-byte write of bytes 0-5
    that replaces it.  The merge copies that read into a temporary, then
    tested the temporary against the pieces of st[0] with Covers that were
    never rebuilt after the pieces were created, so they read as live
    nowhere and the temporary was merged back into byte 5: the printed C
    read ((char *)&v5)[5] after the write, giving byte 7 the new byte 5
    (wrong for every odd x whose bytes 5 and 7 differ).  The pieces'
    Covers are now rebuilt before the test, so byte 5 is saved before the
    write (#1) and byte 7 takes the saved value (#2, #3).
-->
